# Verify representative character, prop, vehicle, interior, and world imports

Verify representative character, prop, vehicle, interior, and world imports.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Verify representative character, prop, vehicle, interior, and world
  imports.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

Open work migrated from the canonical TODO. Add implementation and
validation evidence here as the task advances.

A live September 4 world representative now passes the native import/read-back
transaction. The target-safe `l1z1` world FBX imports through the SHAR editor
toolset, reports one LOD, 30,984 vertices, 17,580 triangles, and 77 material
slots, then rolls back without leaving a package. This proves the repeated-index
target split is accepted by Unreal Engine 5.8.1.

The representative world import now also passes the target surface-frame
contract. The world-only importer consumes the regenerated `l1z1` FBX with
supplied normal, tangent, and binormal frames while preserving authored normals
when present. A fresh native invocation reports zero nearly-zero normal or
binormal warnings, zero degenerate tangent-basis warnings, zero missing
smoothing-group warnings, and zero warnings or errors in the invocation log
segment.

This closes the representative world shading blocker.

A representative character transaction now also passes native import and
rollback. The cataloged Cletus FBX is verified against SHA-256
`e8db1fde82875b05ac14faf95bacc7daaa916749aa201ea70aa75cf2360b7815`
before import. `ImportSkeletalMesh` returns exactly one `SkeletalMesh` and its
new `Skeleton` companion; independent read-back reports the expected classes
and both packages dirty under the no-save contract. Deleting the mesh and then
the skeleton succeeds, and independent existence checks report both absent.

A representative prop transaction now passes the same native import boundary.
The cataloged head-to-head flag FBX is verified against SHA-256
`13b34f060008df50ecf45876125fb4e7cfc0a2f658b568e02a3bc4129eb36e73`
before import. `ImportStaticMesh` returns exactly one `StaticMesh`; independent
read-back reports the expected class and a dirty package under the no-save
contract. Compensating deletion succeeds, and independent asset and temporary
folder existence checks both report absent afterward.

A representative fused interior transaction now passes the world-specific
native import boundary. A complete 129-package replay publishes eight canonical
base interiors and four additive Halloween overlays; the smallest base artifact,
`i07-barts-room`, is verified at 3,182,860 bytes with SHA-256
`86c063afc3fe1bc9b189c68ea59b0964185a4d4d7cf88ea4ffc07bbb56e302bb`
before import. Its catalog records 26 geometries, 26 materials, 26 textures,
zero repeated-index target omissions, seven zero-area target omissions, and
zero removed duplicate triangles.

`ImportWorldStaticMesh` returns exactly one `StaticMesh`. Independent native
read-back reports one LOD, 25,918 render vertices, 9,153 render triangles, 26
material slots, valid local bounds from
`[-503.07611083984375, -456.65597534179688, -20.118066787719727]` to
`[-496.23422241210938, -446.92385864257812, -16.994222640991211]`, and a dirty
package under the no-save contract. Compensating deletion succeeds, and
independent asset and temporary-folder existence checks both report absent.

The broader acceptance criterion remains open only for the representative
vehicle transaction. Vehicle source packages deliberately remain semantic
splits in the generic Unreal manifest; the dedicated vehicle FBX catalog and a
native vehicle construction boundary must preserve that distinction.

A complete dedicated vehicle replay now publishes all 88 vehicle FBXs plus the
vehicle catalog from 89 source packages. The representative `sedana` FBX is
verified at 327,292 bytes with SHA-256
`741f6c43fbbbde89653c501921ece7de9b513261dd8e0be1d80e99abc32b0eac`.

A native no-save presentation transaction imports that FBX as exactly one
`SkeletalMesh` plus one new `Skeleton`. Independent read-back reports one LOD,
896 render vertices, the cataloged 16-bone hierarchy in order, eight semantic
material slots, the expected Skeleton companion, and both packages dirty.

The reviewed skeletal importer intentionally creates no `PhysicsAsset`, and
three sampled vehicle material slots have no material assigned. Compensating
deletion removes the mesh, Skeleton, and temporary folder, with independent
existence checks reporting all three absent afterward.

This proves the dedicated vehicle catalog reaches Unreal's skeletal presentation
boundary without flattening the vehicle into a static-mesh transaction. The
broader acceptance criterion remains open for the native vehicle construction
boundary: Physics Asset, materials, wheel definitions, and Chaos vehicle
composition still require their reviewed typed construction transaction.

A live native `SharVehicles` automation run after the skeletal smoke passes all
three registered tests with zero warnings or errors. Definition validation,
damage runtime, and selection transaction are therefore green; no native
vehicle-construction test or construction object exists in the current module.
This narrows the remaining P0 work to the typed construction boundary rather
than another catalog or skeletal-import repair.

The typed native presentation boundary now exists without changing the
identity/tuning contract in `USharVehicleDefinition`.
`USharVehiclePresentationDefinition` requires the final Skeletal Mesh, Skeleton,
Physics Asset, Animation Blueprint class, ordered Material Instances, semantic
rig profile, deterministic preparation revision, and ordered wheel bindings.
Each wheel binding carries its canonical wheel id, exact skeletal bone, and
native Chaos wheel class. The project registers this definition as the
`SharVehiclePresentation` Primary Asset type, and `SharVehicles` now declares
the `ChaosVehicles` module while the project explicitly enables
`ChaosVehiclesPlugin`.

A fresh Unreal Engine 5.8.1 Linux editor build validates that boundary natively.
The bounded precompiled build compiled all 31 required actions for `shar`,
`SharImportEditor`, and `SharVehicles`, including the new presentation
definition and vehicle automation tests, then linked all three editor modules
with `Result: Succeeded` and no compiler errors.

A project-owned native construction boundary now advances that gap without
claiming final `sedana` assembly. `ASharVehiclePawn` provides the concrete Chaos
wheeled Pawn, while `USharVehicleConstructionTransaction` resolves the reviewed
presentation references before mutation, verifies the exact Skeleton, material
slot count, native wheel classes, and skeletal wheel bones, captures the prior
Pawn configuration, and applies Skeletal Mesh, Physics Asset, animation class,
materials, wheel setups, mass, and engine torque. Commit requires read-back of
those applied fields and rollback restores the captured configuration.

A fresh Unreal Engine 5.8.1 editor build compiled all 33 actions and linked
`SharVehicles` with `Result: Succeeded`. The live
`SHAR.Vehicles.Construction.Transaction` automation test then found exactly one
test, exercised prepare, commit, native field read-back, explicit rollback, and
rejection of a missing wheel bone, and completed with `Result={Success}` and
exit code 0.

The broader P0 criterion remains open for the representative source vehicle.
The bounded test uses transient native assets; it does not yet create reviewed
`sedana` Physics Asset, material, animation, or wheel-definition assets, prove
wheel Physics Asset bodies, spawn the vehicle into a world, create live Chaos
physics state, or validate driven behavior against source evidence.
